Sure. Here is the ReSTIR GI RT algorithm in simple pseudocode, including refraction:

```
procedure ReSTIR(scene, camera, image)

  // Initialize the scene and camera.
  for each object in scene:
    object.init()
  camera.init()

  // Initialize the image.
  for each pixel in image:
    image[pixel].color = black

  // Trace paths.
  for each pixel in image:
    for each sample in pixel:
      NAME_1 = trace(camera, pixel)
      image[pixel].color += NAME_1.color

  // Denoise the image.
  denoise(image)

  // Return the image.
  return image

procedure trace(camera, pixel)

  // Initialize the NAME_1.
  NAME_1 = new NAME_1()
  NAME_1.origin = camera.position
  NAME_1.direction = camera.direction

  // Trace the NAME_1.
  while NAME_1.depth < MAX_DEPTH:
    // Sample a surface.
    hit = scene.intersect(NAME_1)
    if hit is null:
      break

    // Update the NAME_1.
    NAME_1.surface = hit.surface
    NAME_1.position = hit.position
    NAME_1.normal = hit.normal

    // Sample a light.
    light = scene.sampleLight(NAME_1)
    if light is null:
      break

    // Compute the radiance.
    radiance = light.getRadiance(NAME_1)

    // Add the radiance to the NAME_1's color.
    NAME_1.color += radiance * NAME_1.weight

    // Update the NAME_1's weight.
    NAME_1.weight *= NAME_1.surface.getRoughness()

    // Check for refraction.
    if NAME_1.surface.isRefractive:
      refraction = NAME_1.surface.refract(NAME_1.direction)
      if refraction is not null:
        NAME_1.direction = refraction

  // Return the NAME_1.
  return NAME_1
```

This algorithm works by tracing paths from the camera to the scene. Each NAME_1 is initialized with the camera's position and direction. The NAME_1 is then traced through the scene, sampling surfaces and lights along the way. The radiance from each light is added to the NAME_1's color, and the NAME_1's weight is updated based on the surface's roughness. If the NAME_1 hits a refractive surface, the direction of the NAME_1 is refracted. The NAME_1 is traced until it reaches a maximum depth or until it escapes the scene. The colors of all of the paths that reach the image are added together to form the final image.

Voici un exemple de programme en C# qui crée un serveur HTTP à l'aide de la classe `HttpListener` et répond à une demande en affichant "Hello" sur la page.
```
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace HttpServer
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            HttpListener listener = new HttpListener();
            listener.Prefixes.Add("<http://localhost:8080/>");
            listener.Start();

            Console.WriteLine("Server started on port 8080");

            while (true)
            {
                HttpListenerContext context = listener.GetContext();

                if (context.Request.HttpMethod == "GET")
                {
                    byte[] responseBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("Hello");
                    context.Response.StatusCode = 200;
                    context.Response.ContentType = "text/plain";
                    context.Response.ContentLength64 = responseBytes.Length;

                    using (var responseStream = context.Response.OutputStream)
                    {
                        responseStream.Write(responseBytes, 0, responseBytes.Length);
                    }
                }

                context.Response.Close();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Vous pouvez compiler et exécuter ce programme en tapant les commandes suivantes dans la ligne de commande :
```
csc /target:exe HttpServer.cs
HttpServer.exe
```
